"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,225\nEpisode 8\n\n3\n00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:10,156\nThe case for cumulative exams\n\n4\n00:00:12,956 --> 00:00:14,879\nStudents love unit exams.\n\n5\n00:00:15,179 --> 00:00:17,268\nStudy the chapter, take the test,\n\n6\n00:00:17,268 --> 00:00:18,027\nmove on.\n\n7\n00:00:18,507 --> 00:00:21,443\nCumulative exams, which cover everything so far,\n\n8\n00:00:21,463 --> 00:00:23,018\nare much less popular.\n\n9\n00:00:23,395 --> 00:00:25,474\nBut which one helps students remember?\n\n10\n00:00:25,644 --> 00:00:28,501\nIn 2013, Maya Khanna,\n\n11\n00:00:28,772 --> 00:00:35,758\nAmy Badura Brack and Laura Finken compared psychology courses with cumulative and noncumulative finals.\n\n12\n00:00:36,245 --> 00:00:41,763\nStudents who had taken cumulative finals scored higher on later tests of course content,\n\n13\n00:00:42,172 --> 00:00:44,770\neven up to eighteen months after the course.\n\n14\n00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:50,534\nThat same year, Natalie Kudrna Lawrence taught two sections of introductory psychology.\n\n15\n00:00:50,966 --> 00:00:53,561\nOne had cumulative exams all term.\n\n16\n00:00:53,978 --> 00:00:59,785\nStudents in that section did better on the final and on a retention test two months after the course.\n\n17\n00:01:00,237 --> 00:01:02,448\nTheir course grades averaged a B,\n\n18\n00:01:02,673 --> 00:01:04,386\ncompared with a C plus.\n\n19\n00:01:04,556 --> 00:01:11,235\nIn Lawrence's study, the benefit for long term retention showed up mainly for lower scoring students.\n\n20\n00:01:11,705 --> 00:01:16,801\nCumulative exams seemed to push them into habits that high scorers already had.\n\n21\n00:01:16,971 --> 00:01:18,975\nWhy do cumulative exams work?\n\n22\n00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:22,646\nThey require students to return to earlier material,\n\n23\n00:01:22,708 --> 00:01:25,186\nwhich is spacing and retrieval practice,\n\n24\n00:01:25,385 --> 00:01:27,175\nboth from earlier series.\n\n25\n00:01:27,594 --> 00:01:31,281\nMaterial is reviewed, not dropped after the unit test.\n\n26\n00:01:31,451 --> 00:01:32,413\nSome caution.\n\n27\n00:01:32,837 --> 00:01:34,916\nThese were a small number of studies,\n\n28\n00:01:35,016 --> 00:01:37,125\nmostly in psychology courses,\n\n29\n00:01:37,343 --> 00:01:40,449\nand students were not randomly assigned to sections.\n\n30\n00:01:40,818 --> 00:01:43,033\nResults in other subjects may differ.\n\n31\n00:01:43,203 --> 00:01:46,578\nCumulative exams can also feel overwhelming.\n\n32\n00:01:47,012 --> 00:01:50,061\nStudents need help with how to review a whole course,\n\n33\n00:01:50,272 --> 00:01:51,673\nnot just a chapter.\n\n34\n00:01:51,843 --> 00:01:52,632\nFor teaching.\n\n35\n00:01:52,978 --> 00:01:54,565\nMake the final cumulative,\n\n36\n00:01:54,585 --> 00:01:57,718\nand include some earlier material on every exam.\n\n37\n00:01:58,069 --> 00:01:59,415\nTell students in advance,\n\n38\n00:01:59,435 --> 00:02:01,355\nand teach them to review weekly.\n\n39\n00:02:01,734 --> 00:02:04,599\nProvide practice questions from earlier units.\n\n40\n00:02:04,947 --> 00:02:09,096\nOne tip to share with students: review old units every week.\n\n41\n00:02:09,266 --> 00:02:12,327\nWhat gets tested again gets remembered longer.\n\n"